Date: Wed, 20 Jan 1993 00:56:04 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: est/Forum training

I took the basic est training in 1975, along with a number of follow-up
seminars over the next several years, so I feel qualified to comment.  Over
time, I've taken much greater responsibility for my life and consequently am
much more successful.  Would this have happened without the training?  Of
course.  But when?  I credit this effective training for a sharp
acceleration of my personal growth.  It saved me years and was worth far
more to me than what I paid for it.  
 
As for not being allowed to take unscheduled bathroom breaks, everybody knew
and agreed to that rule going into it.  I took that agreement seriously and
planned for it.  Besides, nobody was physically held against their
will--they were merely asked (emphatically) to keep their word.  Growth is
uncomfortable.  When you are unwilling to acknowledge that you are
responsible for creating your life, your body will give you plenty of ways
to avoid paying attention.  Nothing to confront if you're sitting safe in
the rest room, is there?  
 
I won't defend Werner Erhardt's personal conduct, but then, there's no need.
 Whether or not he's a jerk has no bearing on the validity of the training.
It works.  Besides, these ideas are thousands of years old.  Werner never
claimed to originate any of them.  The only thing new was the packaging.
 
I have not been involved since the est training evolved into the Forum, so I
can't speak as to it's like now.  Knowing its roots, however, I have a high
degree of confidence that it would be valuable for most people.  I recommend
checking it out.
 
 
 
