	Hmm ... Looks like the time is ripe for me to go and reread 'The 
Remembered Present' (subtitled 'a biological theory of consciousness') by 
Gerard Edelman (a neurologist/ computational neuroscientist of some note - hes
actually got a Nobel, though not for his neuro work). I seem to recall that I
liked his treatment of the whole subject of consciousness a lot. He had a 
hierarchy of different levels of 'consciousness'. The lowest level of
consciousness was something like ' recognition of the self, and the environment
as different entities, and recognising that each may act upon the other', a 
definition that animals may easily qualify under, and the most complex was 
something like 'having a concept of the self, and recognising and predicting
changes in ones own mental states', a defintion that some humans might not 
qualify under. I'll reread the relevent parts, and post some sort of summary.
	The Edelman book was explicitly talking about consciousness in living
things, but made requirments that the conscious being in question be biolgical.
(He thinks, as far as I can make out, that AI is perfectly reasonable in 
theory, and that all the current practitioners are on the wrong track - except
those that agree with him, of course).
	For another possibly good reference, anybody read the new Daniel
Dennett book, modestly titled 'Consciouness Explained'. If anybody has, I 
wouldn't mind a review, especially from someone with a philosophy background.
Max More maybe?

