From: davidc@cs.uwa.edu.au (David Cake)
Subject: Re: LING/POLI: Western Rationalism?

> 
> 
>    Where does the term "Western" rationalism originate from? I've used
> it myself sometimes, but as far as I can tell, westerners on the average
> are no more or less rational than easterners. They have their rituals,

It is my understanding that the term 'Western' rationalism refers not to all
Western culture (which, indeed, contains just as much silliness as Eastern)
but to a subset of that culture, the twin rises of the scientific method and
of logical deduction. These two are interlinked, but not the same (ie Berkeley
fits well into the logical tradition, but hardly science (though his version
of optics was interesting!)). It does not refer to Christianity, and in fact
may have had a lot to do with its (comparitive) downfall. 
	Of course, I am in no position to judge wether the claim that science
and logic as we know them now are mostly Western creations, or wether this is
just Western hubris. I believe the middle east had more to with it than we let
on. But on the other hand, the logical deduction basis of most Western 
philosophy seems to be the thing that many adherents of Eastern religions are
rejecting, in favour of a more 'holistic' view, so it may be correct.
	
