Date: Wed, 20 Jan 93 13:57:43 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL: Restitution is Usually Not Enough

I wrote:
>Here's the scenario.  I claim "I've
>been robbed!".  The criminal has a week to turn themselves in.  If
>still no criminal then, I then seek the protection/insurance company
>that will pay me the $1000 restitution due, while visiting the least
>punishment on the criminal (a combination of fine due if caught and
>probability of catching them).  The fine that the criminal would pay here
>would then be due to costs of restitution, chasing, prosecution, and
>insurance in case they aren't caught.  No other crime or criminal is
>effected here.  

Tom Morrow responded:
>The problem remains:  insurance costs reflect the risks imposed by all
>criminals--not just those who are caught.  Forcing only those who are
>caught to pay for the victims' insurance demands more than mere
>restitution, since the caught criminal is forced to pay for others' crimes.
>...Maybe I've misunderstood Robin, though.  If so, I'm confident I'll hear
>about it!  8-)

Yes, I think you have misunderstood.  Let's use an analogy.  I own a
road and I require you to get accident insurance before I'll let you
drive on it.  You complain, "That's socialist coercion - I'll have to
pay for costs imposed by people who have accidents, even if I don't
have an accident."

But insurance is not (mainly) a commons.  It is true that with finite
information to base premiums on, accidents that other folks have been
in will influence the premiums that others will charge you.  You are
often tossed in with other people when someone estimates the chance
that something will happen to you.  But competing insurance agencies
have strong incentives to make the best case-specific estimate
possible, distinguishing you from everyone else however possible.  

Thus I don't think that requiring insurance is particularly coercive.
In the criminal example, competing insurers are trying to best
estimate the chance of that particular criminal being caught, and the
amount of money he/she will be able to pay.  Requiring the criminal to
pay for this insurance about themselves seems no more objectionable
than requiring the road driver to buy accident insurance.

I suppose you could make a stand on the position that what makes the
driver insurance requirement acceptable is the fact that in principle
the driver would have the option to post a bond instead of buying
insurance.  The criminal would find this difficult to do.  But I don't
see required insurance as coercion.

Robin Hanson

