Date: Thu, 21 Jan 93 01:19:28 GMT
From: "Stephen J. Whitrow" <sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk>
Subject: Re: The method of Science, the aim of Religion

Ron Hale-Evans brings up the topic of synchronicity, an idea that I have long
been fascinated with. I expect we all come across instances where two or more
events are not causally connected, but appear to have some meaningful
connection - an 'acausal connection'. Many of these are no doubt just
coincidence as the skeptics would claim, and tell us more about how our minds
work than the ultimate nature of reality. I'd like to present some first-hand
evidence on a series of events and connections, and maybe some statistics
experts out there would care to calculate the true improbability of the 
experiences. 

A series of (seemingly) significant connections are listed below, and for each
one I have estimated an 'improbability factor'. Suppose I noted ten connections,
each with a probability of 0.1  . If we were to multiply these together we would
get odds of 10 ^ 10 against, although the fact that the universe contains vastly
more than ten things means that these odds have to be drastically reduced. But
not quite as drastically when you allow that I did not check out everything in
the universe. What I'd like to know is exactly how much the odds are reduced. 

By multiplying together the individual probabilities (or the reciprocal which I
have termed improbabilities) I got odds of 1.016 * 10 ^ 18 to 1 against. To
calculate the true probability accurately you would probably need to know
exactly how many things I considered as a possible meaningful connection and
failed to find a link, in the timescale involved. It's only natural that the
proportion of 'meaningful' to 'non-meaningful' connections would be
overestimated. At the time it only took me a couple of weeks or so to come up
with these alleged acausal connections. But being reasonable, I don't think I
could have considered over 10 ^ 3 to 10 ^ 4 things in this period. Perhaps
1000 and 10000 could be two values assigned to this unknown variable. 

First the background. 1977 was a bad year for me, as far as driving vehicles and
being able to maintain their original shape is concerned. (Fortunately matters
have greatly improved since, I hasten to add!) As a service engineer working on
electronic office equipment, I travelled to a wide range of offices within a 50
mile radius. One day in September of that year I was driving a minivan (a very
small van built by the nationalised company British Leyland) when I lost control
on a bend in the wet and hit a lamppost. Fortunately I was uninjured - the
vehicle was just about written off. (These minivans were crap, IMHO, apart from
being unreliable the handling was _very_ unpredictable. The rear could suddenly
swing round when braking very lightly on a road that was slightly damp. BL was a
company which had communist shop stewards, and its factories featured secret
dormitory compartments incorporating smuggled-in beds, TV sets, armchairs etc
where the nightshift spent much of their working hours. Still, that was the 
company vehicle I'd been given. Apparently in the old days, employees had to go
by bus.)

So now we come to the individual factors which I considered as possibly
something more than mere coincidence. For each one, I have shown my estimated
odds against the event (if taken in isolation which of course it wasn't).

(1) There were three incidents, all with the same minivan, all involving water
and a town named Shepton Mallet. On April 28, 1977 I'd just finished a repair
call at a customer named Showerings, in Shepton Mallet. There had been a very
heavy shower. I tried to start the van, but it wouldn't. There was so much
water in the distributor that I had to get a garage to blow it dry.

On June 21, 1977 the van sprang a water leak and I had to keep stopping to
refill. My repair call that day was at a village some 15 miles beyond Shepton
Mallet, but I drove through the town. By the return journey I was fed up with
having to keep stopping to get water, and called at a garage in Shepton Mallet.
Although it was too late for them to fix it that day, they did give me a
5-gallon drum full of water which was enough to get me home.
  
On September 27, 1977 I was driving through Shepton Mallet, when a bend was too
bent, and the friction coefficient was too low for the vehicle to take at 30mph.
After zigzagging for a brief distance the vehicle spun off to the left, hitting 
a lamppost. 

Now because water is a very common meme, an archetype even, I've only allowed
an improbability of 2 (or 0.5 probability). As for 'Shepton Mallet' being of
significance, much of our calls were in Bristol where the workshop was based.
When we did have out-of-town calls they could be in any direction. Not that
many calls came in for Shepton Mallet or a destination beyond it. I decided
that 0.1 was a reasonable probability for this. There were 3 events, but the
first one would merely decide the common themes. So we square (0.1 * 0.5)
rather than treble it. Or (10 * 2) ^ 2 if we go by the 'improbability'. A
1 in 400 chance is a little easier to picture than 0.0025 probability, so I'll
use this format for the moment. 
                      So our factor # (1) = a 1 in 400 chance.

(2) In all of the above three events, the day of the week was either a Tuesday
or a Thursday - i.e. the first letter was 'T'. And the first digit of the date
was a '2'. The first event gave us 'T' as a common theme - so probability of
this being repeated twice is (2 / 7) ^ 2. I'll be fair here and only include
Mondays to Fridays, as my weekend mileage was small by comparison. We have
(2 / 5) ^ 2.

Given that the first event gave us '2' as the common theme, the chance of any
subsequent date being the twenty-something is approximately 10 / 30.44, or
this squared for the two repeats. So the total for '2' and 'T' becomes
     [(2 / 5) * (10 / 30.44)] ^ 2 = 0.01727
                           Factor # (2) = a 1 in 57.9 chance.
 
(3) Another point is that '2' and 'T' can be derived from 'Shepton Mallet'.
'2' is the number of words in 'Shepton Mallet' and 'T' is the only letter which
appears twice ( '2' times, too!) apart from 'E' and 'L'. However, they are both
ineligible as there are no days of the week beginning with E or L.  
                      I shan't count any improbability here!

(4) We keep the three events of (1) above in chronological order, naming them
a) b) and c). The difference in days between a) and b) is 98, and the difference
between b) and c) is 152. There is something very interesting about the fraction
152 / 98. We get 1.551020408163265 which is a little special in itself, the '15'
reverses itself and then we have a doubling up of 02, 04....  But try raising e
to this power. e ^ x where x = 152 / 98 gives us 4.71628  }  both taken to 6
                                            from 2.71828  }  significant digits
  
We have 4 digits the same and in the correct place in two 6-digit numbers. And
following an exponential operation. I make this a 15 in 10000 chance, or
1 in 667. Moreover, of the remaining two, one has increased by 2 whilst the
other has decreased by 2, so the sum of the 6 digits is the same. Given that
the starting two changed digits were '2' and '8', I make this an 8 in 81
chance. We have 667 * 10.125 for the improbability.
                           Factor # (4) = a 1 in 6753 chance. 

(5) On Monday 26th September I heard that a friend from work had crashed while
driving his own car into town on the Saturday. Quite a bit of damage - and he
hadn't comprehensively insured it. A few of us went to take a look at it during
the dinnerhour. So the 'crash' meme was planted in my mind, and an event
pertaining to the meme occured the very next day. 

O.k., my accident rate was about every 3 months back in '77, including the ones
where it was the other guy's fault. September happened to be the 'right' month,
and we'll assume 20 working days in a month.
                           Factor # (5) = a 1 in 20 chance.

(6) There were a number of similarities between both crashes. (i) Both followed
attempting to take a bend in a way which was beyond the laws of physics. (ii) 
In both cases the road surface was wet (and greasy as the rain was only a
drizzle following a dry spell). (iii) In both cases the vehicles spun to the
left. (iv) In both cases some type of post was hit - in his case a bus stop, in
my case a lamp post. For each of these I have allowed a nominal 1 in 2
improbability.
                           Factor # (6) = a 1 in 16 chance.

(7) It was a full moon on September 27, 1977, as shown in my diary. 
                           Factor # (7) = a 1 in 29 chance.

(8) On the night of September 26, 1977 I'd just happened to make a major
rearrangement of the furniture in my bedroom, bringing in a larger wardrobe. It 
was the first major change for 23 months.
                           Factor # (8) = a 1 in 700 chance.

(9) Subtract 1000 days from September 27, 1977. The date is January 1st, 1975.
I've allowed 1 in 100 for the chance of a New Years' Day being separated by a
'round number' of days. 
                           Factor # (9) = a 1 in 100 chance.

(10) The number of days I had the company minivan (until its demise) was 344.
The last engineer who'd had it, had it for 217 days since it had been
reallocated to him. If you divide 344 / 217 the quotient is the fifth root of
ten, accurate to four figures. I allowed a nominal 1 in 10 here.
                           Factor # (10) = a 1 in 10 chance.

So now we multiply all ten factors together. This gives us a total
improbability factor of about 1.016 * 10 ^ 18. Now if I'd only looked at ten
factors as possible acausal connections, and all ten had been the above hits,
this would prove the case for synchronicity as the odds against it would be as
above. Given that I found no (apparently) significant connection with the vast
majority of things that I investigated for possible synchronicities, I'd like
to know whether this totally reduces the improbability to a state of normality.
So the ten 'significant' factors above must have been scattered amongst
10 ^ 3 to 10 ^ 4 'insignificant' factors.

Let's just show these 'improbabilities' together:-

400                    Perhaps it makes more sense to add these when amongst
57.9                   many insignificant 'misses'. If you considered the
6753                   results of 8000 horse races, it might seem reasonable
20                     to expect a winner with odds of 6750 / 1 to turn up
16                     once, followed by others at 700-1, 400-1, 100-1 etc.
