The extreme mystic can always claim that certain unlikely events 'prove' the
validity of synchronicity, and the skeptic can always respond that with so
many things in the world some of them will seem 'improbable'. I think we can
be a bit more precise. 

I could also use the approach of excluding the irrelevant factors above such as
'full moon' and 'change of furniture'. This should cut the number of
'insignificant' factors noticeably with a much smaller reduction of the 
'improbability' odds.

Jung's case of the scarabaeid beetle just happening to knock on the window and
fly in as his patient was recounting her dream about the golden scarab certainly
sounds like a high odds against event - I wonder if anyone has computed the
improbability of this.

